::ENG1_U02_PRE_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.R.01] Passage: The hallway light flickered above the abandoned trophy case, and Camila saw one gold cup turned backward as if hiding its name.
Which literary element most shapes the mysterious feeling? {
=A. Setting#Correct. The abandoned trophy case, flickering light, and hidden name create a mysterious atmosphere.
~B. Rhyme#Incorrect. The passage is prose and does not rely on rhyme.
~C. Dialogue#Incorrect. No one speaks in this passage.
~D. Stage directions#Incorrect. The passage is narrative prose, not a script.
}

::ENG1_U02_PRE_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.R.02] Passage: Priya wanted to quit after the first failed attempt. Instead, she saved the broken model, studied where it had bent, and built a stronger bridge the next day.
Which theme is best supported? {
~A. Bridges are impossible to build.#Incorrect. Priya builds a stronger bridge, so this is not supported.
~B. Quitting is always the best solution.#Incorrect. Priya does not quit.
~C. Models should never be studied.#Incorrect. Priya studies the model to improve.
=D. Failure can lead to improvement when people learn from it.#Correct. Priya uses the failed model to build something stronger.
}

::ENG1_U02_PRE_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.R.03] Passage: I smiled when Dad said we were moving again. I smiled so hard my cheeks hurt, and then I went upstairs to pack the posters I had never finished hanging.
What does the narration reveal? {
~A. The narrator is completely happy about moving.#Incorrect. The painful smile and unfinished posters suggest hidden sadness.
=B. The narrator's outward reaction hides disappointment.#Correct. The narrator smiles, but details show the move hurts.
~C. The narrator knows Dad is joking.#Incorrect. The passage gives no sign that the move is a joke.
~D. The narrator is speaking about someone else's feelings.#Incorrect. First-person narration reveals the narrator's own experience.
}

::ENG1_U02_PRE_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.R.09] Passage: The secret spread through the lunchroom like a spark racing across dry grass.
What does the simile suggest? {
~A. The secret disappeared immediately.#Incorrect. A spark racing across dry grass suggests fast spreading, not disappearing.
~B. The secret was carefully protected.#Incorrect. The image suggests uncontrolled movement.
~C. The secret was about a fire drill.#Incorrect. The comparison is figurative, not proof of the secret's topic.
=D. The secret spread quickly and dangerously.#Correct. The spark image suggests speed and possible harm.
}

::ENG1_U02_PRE_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.R.04] Passage: The queen crossed the battlefield alone, carrying no weapon except the banner of her people.
Which epic quality is suggested? {
~A. A quiet scene about ordinary chores#Incorrect. The battlefield and banner create a larger heroic situation.
~B. A humorous misunderstanding at dinner#Incorrect. The passage is serious and elevated.
=C. A leader representing a community in a high-stakes moment#Correct. The queen's action and banner connect personal courage to her people.
~D. A technical explanation of banners#Incorrect. The banner is symbolic, not technically explained.
}

::ENG1_U02_PRE_Q06::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Original story: A character opens a forbidden door and discovers a truth about the family. Adaptation: A student opens a hidden folder on an old computer and discovers a truth about the family.
What has the adaptation changed? {
=A. The object and setting used to reveal the truth#Correct. The forbidden door becomes a hidden computer folder, changing how the truth is found.
~B. The basic discovery pattern#Incorrect. Both versions keep the discovery pattern.
~C. The fact that a truth is discovered#Incorrect. Both versions include discovery of a truth.
~D. The focus on family#Incorrect. Both versions still involve family.
}

::ENG1_U02_PRE_Q07::[MLA.ENG1.W.01] Passage: Mina held the envelope under the porch light. Her name was written in blue ink, but no return address appeared in the corner.
Which sentence best continues the narrative with suspense? {
~A. Envelopes are used to send letters through the mail.#Incorrect. This is informational and stops the narrative.
~B. Mina tossed it aside and forgot it forever.#Incorrect. This removes the suspense instead of developing it.
=C. Mina turned the envelope over slowly, listening to the porch boards creak beneath her feet.#Correct. This keeps the point of view and builds suspense with action and sound.
~D. You should always check mail carefully.#Incorrect. This shifts into advice and second person.
}

::ENG1_U02_PRE_Q08::[MLA.ENG1.R.01] Passage: The old piano sat in the center of the gym, its keys yellowed but uncovered. Around it, folding chairs formed a silent circle.
Which detail most suggests that the piano is important? {
=A. The keys are yellowed but uncovered.#Correct. The contrast suggests age but also attention, making the piano stand out.
~B. The gym exists.#Incorrect. The location matters, but it does not alone show the piano's importance.
~C. Folding chairs can be moved.#Incorrect. The passage does not focus on moving chairs.
~D. The piano is old, so it cannot matter.#Incorrect. The passage suggests the old piano may still be important.
}

::ENG1_U02_PRE_Q09::[MLA.ENG1.R.09] Passage: Her voice was a thin thread holding the room together.
What does the metaphor suggest? {
~A. Her voice is literally made of thread.#Incorrect. This is figurative language, not a literal description.
~B. Her voice is unimportant to the scene.#Incorrect. The image shows her voice holds the room together.
~C. Her voice is loud and angry.#Incorrect. "Thin thread" suggests delicacy, not loud anger.
=D. Her voice is fragile but important.#Correct. A thin thread suggests something delicate that still has an important function.
}

::ENG1_U02_PRE_Q10::[MLA.ENG1.W.01] Passage: Jonah waited at the starting line. The whistle hung from the coach's hand, and every runner leaned forward.
Which sentence best uses a transition to continue the sequence? {
~A. Running is a sport with many health benefits.#Incorrect. This changes from narrative to informational writing.
=B. Seconds later, the whistle cut through the air, and Jonah's feet struck the track.#Correct. "Seconds later" provides sequence and continues the action.
~C. Jonah was a student who liked school.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not continue the race moment.
~D. You should stretch before a race.#Incorrect. This shifts into advice and second person.
}
